    <div class="prose">
      <p class="reveal">The concept of environmental, social and governance (ESG) has long been conflated with corporate social responsibility (CSR). However, while the two concepts share the intention of promoting responsible corporate behaviour, they are not interchangeable.</p>
      <p class="reveal">ESG is now emerging as a core operational priority as businesses turn to this data-driven and measurable framework to evaluate their non-financial risks, financial performance, and long-term viability.</p>
      <p class="reveal">Despite the growing importance of ESG, the region faces a reality check. As our recent research in <a href="http://bt.sg/poweringasean2026" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Powering Asean’s Future 2026</a> shows, South-east Asia stands at a juncture. Sustainability is running headlong into short-term financial pressures and compressed margins and rising energy costs are forcing many companies to delay their decarbonisation targets.</p>
      <p class="reveal">The transition itself has not stopped. We see this commitment exhibited by companies and recognise it annually through our Sustainability Impact Awards, organised by The Business Times and UOB, with the Centre for Governance and Sustainability (CGS) at NUS Business School as our knowledge partner.</p>
      <p class="reveal">We now want to take that evaluation a step further. The Business Times has partnered CGS to launch the Asia Pacific Sustainability Index (APSI).</p>
      <p class="reveal">Evaluating corporate sustainability disclosures across 14 distinct markets, which span mature regulatory regimes to markets with less established compliance frameworks, presents a genuine challenge for investors and business leaders.</p>
      <p class="reveal">The APSI addresses this through a contextualised methodology. It evaluates 700 listed companies against the MAGNET framework to determine a base score, then applies a context factor to account for local regulatory requirements. This separates a company’s actual disclosure quality from its jurisdiction-wide mandates.</p>
      <p class="reveal">The findings establish a clear baseline: sustainability reporting has achieved scale across Asia Pacific, but depth remains uneven. While broad emissions pledges are common, substantive board oversight, detailed climate scenario modelling, and credible Scope 3 accounting remain scarce.</p>
      <p class="reveal">We hope this index serves as a clear, unvarnished guide for tracking progress in regional corporate transparency.</p>
      <p class="signoff reveal"><b>Mindy Tan</b><span>BT Insights Editor</span></p>
